00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.800 align:start position:0%
these king penguins live on a small  island in the Crozet archipelago halfway  between the southern tip of Africa and  Antarctica here in the Southern Ocean  the penguin is at the top of the food  chain so scientists study populations  like this to gauge how climate change is  affecting the whole southern ocean  ecosystem to keep track of individual  Birds they use flipper bands which the  birds soon get used to wearing or so it  was assumed but a ten year study now  shows that banded birds produce fewer  chicks and have a lower survival rate  than their unbanded neighbors this casts  doubt on data collected from banded  penguins jeopardising attempts to use  this data to assess the impact of  climate change on marine ecosystems the  researchers followed the fate of a  hundred king penguins from 1998 to 2008  each penguin had a minut transponder tag  inserted under its skin using these tags  together with a system of antennas and  video cameras the researchers were able  to monitor their penguins as they move  between the island breeding site and the  sea half of the penguins also wore  conventional flipper bands these metal  bands are easy to spot at a distance  using binoculars but compared with the  transponder tags they're heavy and they  damage the flipper  the team noticed that the banded birds  arrived later for breeding and spent  more time at scene foraging for food in  the water the metal band seems to  increase drag which affects the  hydrodynamics of the penguin  this makes swimming less efficient and  more tiring so it takes longer to catch  food and this means the banded birds  chicks get less to eat over the decade  the researchers found that the banded  penguins have 39 percent fewer chicks  and a 16 percent slower survival rate if  the way we monitor penguin populations  changes how they behave there's a bias  in the data researchers may need to find  less harmful ways of identifying and  following individual animals and where  we use data collected from banded  penguins to make conclusions about  climate change we must take into account  the flip side of flipper banding
 

